        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: the train's initial speed (50 miles per hour) and the time it took to reach the destination (4 hours). The unknown was the time it would take if the train traveled at 100 miles per hour instead.\n\nFirst, I calculated the distance to the destination using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} \\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = 50 \\, \\text{miles/hour} \\times 4 \\, \\text{hours} = 200 \\, \\text{miles} \\]\n\nNext, I used the distance to find the new time at the increased speed of 100 miles per hour:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} \\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{200 \\, \\text{miles}}{100 \\, \\text{miles/hour}} = 2 \\, \\text{hours} \\]\n\nTo verify the result, I checked that the distance traveled at 100 miles per hour in 2 hours is indeed 200 miles:\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = 100 \\, \\text{miles/hour} \\times 2 \\, \\text{hours} = 200 \\, \\text{miles} \\]\n\nThe operations I used are directly applicable when the speed and time relationship needs to be recalculated for a different speed, given a fixed distance. This method is reusable in similar problems where the distance remains constant but the speed changes.\n\nFINAL: 2",
